BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 454.87 Td (In SSDs, there are no spinning platters. Instead, data is stored across multiple NAND flash chips \(often exposed) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 441.47 Td (as separate targets inside the SSD\) and managed by an NVMe controller \(as shown above\). The important thing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 428.07 Td (is: even on SSDs, the OS still talks to storage in terms of fixed-size blocks \(e.g., 4KB\) using logical block) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 414.67 Td (addresses.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 394.77 Td (So when the database requests an 8KB page, the OS issues reads for the corresponding 2 blocks. The NVMe) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 381.37 Td (controller then routes those block reads to the correct target / flash chip, fetches the data from the underlying) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 367.97 Td (flash pages, and returns the bytes back into RAM. From the database\222s point of view, the page-based model) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 354.57 Td (stays the same only the physical storage mechanics under the hood change.) Tj ET
