BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 614.15 Td (When I was working as a Junior Software Engineer in my early days, I\222d sit in meetings where people casually) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 600.75 Td (talked about \223scalability\224 of our systems. I honestly thought it just meant \223Can our database survive more traffic) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 587.35 Td (without crashing?\224 and left it at that.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 567.45 Td (Over time I realised people were really asking a deeper question:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 547.55 Td (As the business and the app grow, can the database not only survive, but also keep up and stay fast?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 527.65 Td (Scalability isn\222t only about scaling up when demand spikes. Good systems also let you scale down when traffic) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 514.25 Td (is low, so you\222re not burning money on overkill infrastructure at 2 a.m.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 494.35 Td (When a database can\222t scale, it usually fails in a few predictable ways:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 474.45 Td (CPU / memory get overloaded \226 queries slow down, timeouts appear, and the database starts thrashing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 454.55 Td (Storage gets tight \226 disks hit capacity, writes fail, or you have to scramble for emergency cleanup.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 434.65 Td (The network becomes a bottleneck \226 too much data moving in and out, and everything feels sluggish.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 414.75 Td (In this article, we\222ll walk through the core horizontal and vertical scaling strategies.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 394.85 Td (By the end, you should have a clear mental model of when to use each approach and how they fit together as) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 381.45 Td (your app and traffic grow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 361.55 Td (Thanks for reading LevelYouUp! Subscribe for free to receive new posts and support my work.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 58.00 328.65 Td (What is Vertical Scaling?) Tj ET
