BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 344.00 Td (Horizontal scaling is the process of adding more machines and spreading the workload across them and in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 330.60 Td (context of db it is spreading the database workload across them.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 310.70 Td (In databases, this usually shows up as:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 290.80 Td (Read replicas) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 270.90 Td (Shards) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 251.00 Td (Split workloads) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 231.10 Td (The natural question for anybody to ask would be \(which even i asked myself\), why would horizontal scaling in) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 217.70 Td (database show up like that and why not similar to the Horizontal Scaling in Backend Services ? And that\222s a) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 204.30 Td (very good question.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 184.40 Td (For backend services, horizontal scaling is simple: you add more app servers behind a load balancer, and any) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 171.00 Td (server can handle any request. The servers are mostly stateless, so it doesn\222t really matter which one you hit.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 151.10 Td (Databases are different because they store the actual truth of your data.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 131.20 Td (You can\222t just spin up 5 separate database servers and let all of them accept writes on their own. Very quickly,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 117.80 Td (they would disagree about the data meaning the data would not be consistent across all these servers that way,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 104.40 Td (for example, one server might think your balance is Rs.500, another might think it\222s Rs.700. We want the database) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 91.00 Td (to be consistent, meaning every user and every server should see the same \223truth.\224) Tj ET
