BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 592.09 Td (That\222s kinda the right intuition\205 but it hides a ton of engineering complexity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 572.19 Td (Because if we literally updated the data files and all indexes on every commit, we\222d be:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 552.29 Td (Doing many random writes all over disk.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 532.39 Td (Taking a big performance hit.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 512.49 Td (Still vulnerable to partial writes in the middle of a crash.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 492.59 Td (So how do databases stay fast and safe?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 472.69 Td (Thanks for reading LevelYouUp! Subscribe for free to receive new posts and support my work.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 58.00 439.79 Td (2. Enter WAL: Log First, Data Later) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 419.19 Td (The standard trick is the Write\226Ahead Log \(WAL\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 399.29 Td (WAL is a standard method for ensuring data integrity. It\222s not just used in traditional RDBMS the idea can be) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 385.89 Td (used anywhere we care about reliability and crash recovery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 365.99 Td (The core idea is beautifully simple:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 346.09 Td (Before making changes to the actual database files, log those changes in a separate, append-only log file.) Tj ET
