q 479.00 0 0 277.18 58.00 495.82 cm /Im42 Do Q
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 486.82 Td (Each node in a database system keeps an append-only log file in persistent storage and every client update sent) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 473.42 Td (to the node is first appended to this file \(write-ahead\) and later applied to the data in the database. This logging) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 460.02 Td (technique is called Write-Ahead Logging because the update commands are written to the logs before the actual) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 446.62 Td (updates on the data are performed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 426.72 Td (Think of them like this -) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 406.82 Td (The Database: The official, heavy ledger book where you neatly write final balances and long-term records. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 393.42 Td (WAL: A scratchpad where you quickly jot down every transaction as it happens, in order, before you calmly copy) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 380.02 Td (them into the ledger later.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 360.12 Td (Why is this powerful?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 340.22 Td (The WAL is a sequential, append-only file. Appending to the end is much cheaper than jumping around updating) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 326.82 Td (different parts of tables and indexes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 306.92 Td (Updating the main data files can be done later, in the background.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 287.02 Td (As long as the change is safely written to the WAL, the database can say:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 267.12 Td (\223Commit successful and your data is durable.\224) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 247.22 Td (So instead of:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 227.32 Td (\223Every commit = update all relevant data/index blocks on disk\224) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 207.42 Td (so the entire process would look somewhat like this :) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 187.52 Td (Log the change to WAL \(sequential write\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 167.62 Td (Flush WAL to disk \(ensure it\222s persisted\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 147.72 Td (Writing to the WAL file is not the same as having it safely on disk.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 127.82 Td (There are layers:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 107.92 Td (Your DB writes to its own WAL buffer in RAM.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 88.02 Td (OS writes that to the page cache in RAM.) Tj ET
