BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 778.00 Td (Only when the OS actually issues a disk write + flush do the bytes land on physical storage.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 758.10 Td (If the power goes out while the bytes are only in RAM \(DB buffer or OS cache\) -> they\222re gone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 738.20 Td (So on COMMIT, the DB must ensure:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 718.30 Td (The WAL bytes for this transaction are forced to disk.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 698.40 Td (This is often done with something like fsync\(\) \(or equivalent\), which tells the OS:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 678.50 Td (\223No seriously, push this to stable storage now.\224) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 658.60 Td (Key idea: A transaction is only considered durable when the WAL records for it have definitely hit disk.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 638.70 Td (Mark transaction as committed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 618.80 Td (Later, apply those changes to the actual data files during a background process \(checkpoint\).) Tj ET
BT /F2 10.5 Tf 58.00 587.90 Td (3. Advantages of WAL) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 570.25 Td (Fewer disk writes -> better performance \(Updating table data and indexes directly can mean a lot of random I/O\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 550.35 Td (Point-in-time recovery \(PITR\) \(Incase of failure, can restore a backup of the database.\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 10.5 Tf 58.00 519.45 Td (4. But what if the crash happens while writing the WAL?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 501.80 Td (Good question and it\222s not just theoretical. Imagine we\222re appending a record to the WAL:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 481.90 Td (The record is, say, 100 bytes long.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 462.00 Td (The system crashes after 43 bytes are written.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 442.10 Td (Now we have a truncated / half-written log record. How does the database know:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 422.20 Td (whether this record is valid or corrupted?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 402.30 Td (whether it should replay it during recovery?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 382.40 Td (This is where data protection inside WAL itself comes in.) Tj ET
BT /F2 10.5 Tf 58.00 351.50 Td (5. CRC-32: Guarding each log record) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 333.85 Td (Every serious implementation of WAL protects each record with a checksum. A common choice is CRC-32) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 320.45 Td (\(Cyclic Redundancy Check\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 300.55 Td (Rough idea:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 280.65 Td (When the WAL record is created:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 260.75 Td (The system calculates a CRC-32 checksum of its contents.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 240.85 Td (That checksum is stored alongside the record.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 220.95 Td (During:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 201.05 Td (Crash recovery, or) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 181.15 Td (Replication \(streaming WAL to another server\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 161.25 Td (The database:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 141.35 Td (Recomputes the CRC-32 of the record.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 121.45 Td (Compares it with the stored checksum.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 101.55 Td (If the checksums don\222t match?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 81.65 Td (The record is considered corrupted / incomplete.) Tj ET
