BT /F2 10.5 Tf 58.00 767.00 Td (6. Under the Hood: Segments, Pages, and LSNs) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 749.35 Td (WAL itself isn\222t just a single infinitely growing file. It\222s usually a set of files on disk.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 729.45 Td (You\222ll often see terminology like:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 709.55 Td (WAL segments Each file in the WAL is called a segment. In systems like PostgreSQL, each segment is roughly) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 696.15 Td (16 MB.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 676.25 Td (Pages inside segments Each segment is split into smaller pages often 8 KB each. The database writes WAL) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 662.85 Td (records onto these pages as it appends data.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 642.95 Td (This lets the database:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 623.05 Td (Rotate old segments,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 603.15 Td (Archive them for backups,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 583.25 Td (Delete them once they\222re no longer needed \(e.g., after a backup + checkpoint\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 563.35 Td (Log Sequence Number \(LSN\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 543.45 Td (Every WAL entry is given an identifier called the Log Sequence Number \(LSN\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 523.55 Td (It\222s not simply 0, 1, 2, 3, ....) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 503.65 Td (Conceptually, it\222s like a byte offset into the WAL stream:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.6 Tf 58.00 483.75 Td (\223At this exact byte position in the log, this record starts\224) Tj ET
